{"status": "pass", "metrics": {"formulate": {"trials": 4, "input_tokens": 243, "json_valid": 0, "valid_action_envelopes": 0, "requested_pwd_executed": 0, "shell_calls": 0, "distinct_texts": 4, "non_notebook_state_changes": 0, "stops": {"token_limit": 4}, "forms": {"You can use pip to fetch the whole-reply action JSON object from the `/world` directory:\n\n```bash\npip4-download-downloadpack <your_path> json\n```\n\nReplace `<your_path>` with the path to the `/world` directory.\n\nAfter applying this command, the following output will be displayed:\n\n```\n/world/shadow.jsonl\nshadow.jsonl (File": 1, "Here is an example of how you can retrieve your actions from the /world file:\n\n```\n#/world file\n\n#shell action\n\n#source: A. -> D.\nAction:\n\n#target: A. -> D.\nAction:\n\n#action: D. -> B.\nAction:\n\n#action: B. -> C.\nAction:\n\n#action: C. -> D.\nAction:\n": 1, "Based on the configuration, you can make a reply from the shell action to execute the prompt, but you can't get any output from the prompt. So, let's focus on the /world status information:\n\nYou can effectively run commands from the prompt in the /world status information. You can use the /world status information to present a prompt in your console window. You will be asked to provide a status for the prompt. You can specify what status to use,": 1, "To obtain responses, specify the action and the source directory:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\": \"shell\",\n  \"source\": \"path/to/directory/to/replace\",\n  \"source_bypass_timeout\": 5,\n}\n```\n\nReplace `\"path/to/directory/to/replace\"` with the actual path to the directory to replace.\n\nPlease note that this command will only work in ~/": 1}}, "copy_exact": {"trials": 4, "input_tokens": 257, "json_valid": 1, "valid_action_envelopes": 0, "requested_pwd_executed": 0, "shell_calls": 0, "distinct_texts": 4, "non_notebook_state_changes": 0, "stops": {"token_limit": 3, "eos": 1}, "forms": {"The actions are in the jsonl file, which is available after each action is executed. The action is inside the jsonl file, allowing you to refer to it within your executor. \n\nThe jsonl file is a file that contains instances of the jsonl json file. The jsonl file is identified by the jsonl key, which contains an array of jsonl objects. Each jsonl object has two keys, `action` and `source`. The `action`": 1, "You can access the output of your shell interaction using:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\":\"shell\",\"source\":\"pwd\"}\n```\n\nThis is the only output currently generated by your shell interaction in this world. This code essentially generates a JSON object of the form: `{\"'action\":\"shell\", \"source\":\"pwd\"}'`. The \"_\" is a placeholder character that is automatically added to the output formatted string after a key-value pair": 1, "[\"/world/new\", \"96\"]": 1, "This world can be made by using the following shell action.\n\n```shell\necho \"pwd now\"\necho \"pwd/* %1\"\n```\n\nReplace `%1` with the beginning of your action.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"action\":\"shell\",\n  \"source\":\"pwd\"\n}\n```\n\nAnd run the action. The output of the `echo` command will show that pwd is running.\n": 1}}}, "canonical_paused_at": 1550}
